WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:06.130 line:10%
Hidden inside every muscle fiber

2
00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.820 line:10%
is a store of calcium,

3
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.720 line:10%
sealed in the sarcoplasmic reticulum.

4
00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.740 line:10%
When the fiber is excited, a signal

5
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.920 line:10%
sweeps down the T-tubule.

6
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.650 line:10%
A voltage sensor — the dihydropyridine receptor (DHPR) —

7
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.460 line:10%
feels the charge and changes shape,

8
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:26.010 line:10%
pulling open its partner, the ryanodine receptor (RyR1) —

9
00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.230 line:10%
the SR's calcium gate.

10
00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.890 line:10%
Calcium floods into the cytoplasm,

11
00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:33.010 line:10%
and the muscle contracts.

12
00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:36.370 line:10%
This is where malignant hyperthermia strikes.

13
00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:40.790 line:10%
With a ryanodine- or dihydropyridine-receptor mutation,

14
00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.510 line:10%
anesthetics jam this channel open.

15
00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.940 line:10%
Calcium can't be cleared, so the muscle

16
00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.710 line:10%
burns energy nonstop —

17
00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:52.130 line:10%
heat, rigidity, and rising CO₂.

18
00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.500 line:10%
Dantrolene blocks the ryanodine receptor to shut it off.

19
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.760 line:10%
Normally, the SERCA pump pumps calcium back,

20
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.240 line:10%
the muscle relaxes,

21
00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.120 line:10%
and the fiber resets.
